          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which chapter holds high blood pressure, and what does that chapter deliberately exclude?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure is classified as code I10 in Chapter 9, the circulatory system chapter (I00-I99). This comes from the coding summaries *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family* and *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system*. The same chapter also holds the code for low blood pressure.\n\nThe chapter leaves out several things:\n\n- **A raised reading without a diagnosis.** One high reading, or several, in someone who has not been diagnosed with hypertension is treated as a finding. It goes to Chapter 18 (abnormal findings) instead. The diagnosis in the record decides the code, not the number.\n- **Hypertension during pregnancy, childbirth, or the period after delivery.** This is excluded outright and classified in the obstetric chapter. The pregnancy code is used in place of I10.\n- **Blood vessel problems in the brain or eye caused by hypertension.** These are separate conditions. They may be coded in addition to hypertension, not instead of it.\n\nThese sources only cover how conditions are classified for record-keeping. They do not explain blood pressure targets, symptoms, or treatment. For questions about your own readings, please talk to a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
